
# ESD Visitboard — Unified Prototype Implementation Prompt
## Single, Manageable Backend + Frontend Build Spec (v1 Prototype)

**Purpose of this document:** everything discussed so far — the sandbox feedback transcript, the Tier 1/2/2.5/3 calendar-evidence doctrine, and the deep architecture/CV research report — gets distilled here into **one buildable, low-complexity prototype spec**. The goal is not to implement every mechanism described in the research report at once. It's to ship a small, coherent system that a single developer can maintain, that already encodes the lab's real constraints correctly, and that has clean seams to grow into the fuller Graph-API/CSP system later without a rewrite.

This is written as a direct engineering prompt. Copy sections into your issue tracker or hand them to a code-generation agent as-is.

---

## 1. Design principle: one small backend, one small frontend, one obvious data flow

Everything reviewed so far (the tiering doctrine, the OpenCV pipeline, the CSP/scoring engine, the DB schema) is **correct in substance** but was scoped for a mature, multi-module system (`esd_scheduler/` with `feasibility.py`, `evidence.py`, `scoring.py`, `drift.py`, `store.py`, `calendarsync.py`, `cli.py`, plus a computer-vision microservice). That's the right end state. It is **not** the right v1 prototype, because too many moving modules is exactly what makes a solo-maintained lab tool fall over.

Collapse it into this shape instead:

```
esd_scheduler/
├── app.py                 # single FastAPI app: routes + startup wiring
├── models.py               # SQLAlchemy models (one file, all tables)
├── db.py                   # engine/session setup, SQLite for prototype
├── constraints.py          # hard-constraint filters (Section 3 of the research report)
├── scoring.py               # soft-constraint cost function (Section 3)
├── vision.py                # OpenCV grid + saturation pipeline (Section 4)
├── seed_data.py             # one-time loader: staff, assessments, reliability matrix
└── static/
    ├── index.html           # existing Netlify dashboard, extended
    ├── sync-review.html      # new: calendar-sync review screen
    └── app.js
```

That's it — **six Python files and a static folder.** No microservice split, no `drift.py`/`ranking.py`/`report.py` yet, no CLI tool, no separate ingestion service for the PDF path. Everything from the two prior deep-dive docs is either folded into one of these six files or explicitly deferred to §7 (Roadmap) rather than built now.

**Why this collapse is safe:** the core insight that survives from the tiering doctrine — a Layer-1 feasibility check must be able to say "I don't know" instead of guessing — doesn't require four separate modules. It requires exactly one function, `constraints.is_feasible(candidate, evidence)`, that returns `feasible | infeasible | insufficient_evidence`, and one rule about which evidence sources are allowed to return anything other than `insufficient_evidence`. That fits in `constraints.py` at prototype scale.

