## 2. Stack choice (optimized for "easy to manage and maintain," not for scale)

| Layer | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Backend framework | **FastAPI** (Python) | Async-friendly for the sync-loop, automatic OpenAPI docs for free, and it's the same language as the OpenCV pipeline — no cross-language service boundary to maintain. |
| Database | **SQLite** via SQLAlchemy | Zero ops. A single `.db` file you can back up by copying it. Upgrading to Postgres later is a one-line connection-string change because SQLAlchemy is already the abstraction layer. |
| Computer vision | **OpenCV (`opencv-python-headless`) + NumPy**, in-process | No separate microservice for the prototype — call `vision.py` functions directly from the FastAPI route. A network hop between "upload calendar image" and "process it" buys you nothing at this scale and doubles your deployment surface. |
| Frontend | **Plain HTML/JS on top of your existing Netlify site**, calling the FastAPI backend over CORS-enabled REST | You already have a working, "beautiful" frontend per your own note — don't rebuild it. Add two new pieces: the "Sync Calendars" button/panel and the block-review screen. |
| Hosting | Backend on a small always-on box or a free-tier Python host (Render/Fly.io/Railway); frontend stays on Netlify as-is | Keeps the thing you already shipped untouched; the new backend is additive. |

This directly answers "easy to manage and maintain": one language end-to-end (Python + vanilla JS), one file per concern, one database file, no message queue, no microservices, no OCR unless a fallback path is actually triggered.

